  "articleBody": "Over-sanding drywall is a super common mistake that can actually create more work than it saves. It’s easy to get carried away, especially when you’re aiming for that perfectly smooth surface. The biggest issue with over-sanding is that you can sand right through the joint compound and expose the drywall tape underneath, or even worse, damage the paper facing of the drywall itself. Once you hit the tape, you’ve essentially undone all your hard mudding work, and you’ll likely need to re-mud that area. To avoid this, remember to always use light pressure. You’re not trying to aggressively remove material, just knock down any ridges and blend the edges. It’s also crucial to ensure the compound is completely dry before you start sanding. Sanding too early when the compound is still a bit soft will just gum up your sandpaper and create an uneven mess. Use a proper sanding sponge or a pole sander with fine-grit sandpaper for the final passes. And always check your work by hand and with an angled light – this helps you feel and see any imperfections without having to sand excessively. If you're finding it tricky to get that just-right smooth feel without damaging anything, getting a professional opinion can save you a lot of hassle. We can help you get it right the first time!",
